diff --git a/doc/filters.texi b/doc/filters.texi
index 3534c87c..f687423b 100644
--- a/doc/filters.texi
+++ b/doc/filters.texi
@@ -12026,6 +12026,126 @@ pattern. This is to be used if the stream is cut. The default value is @code{0}.
 @end table
 
 @anchor{dilation}
+@section dibr
+
+Render stereo from a picture and its depth map (depth-image-based
+rendering): the output is full side by side, both views at the picture's
+size, the left eye first, marked as side by side stereo.
+
+It accepts two inputs: the picture, and the depth map, whose first plane is
+read at any size and bit depth. libstereo-dibr does the work, the same core
+the other programs of Sparky Stereo OS use, so FFmpeg is configured with
+@code{--enable-libstereodibr}.
+
+What a depth sample means comes from the depth frame's side data when
+@option{depth} is @code{auto}: the depth representation information of H.264
+(H.13.2.3) and H.265 (G.14.3.4), from their SEI messages or an HEIF depth
+image, with its ZNear and ZFar, or DMin and DMax, and the non-linear
+disparity's DepthLUT; or the supplemental information of an ISO/IEC 23002-3
+auxiliary video stream, a depth map with its kfar and knear, or a parallax
+map with its parallax_zero, parallax_scale and wref. ISO/IEC 23002-3 is not
+freely available: those two are read as open implementations read them, and
+are unverified. Without side data, larger samples are nearer
+(2D-plus-depth).
+
+This filter accepts the following options:
+
+@table @option
+@item view
+What the picture is.
+@table @samp
+@item left
+The left eye: only the right eye is drawn. This is the default.
+@item center
+The middle: both eyes are drawn, half the parallax each way.
+@end table
+
+@item warp
+How the picture is warped into a view.
+@table @samp
+@item forward
+Each pixel is splatted to where it goes, the nearest kept. This is the
+default.
+@item backward
+The depth is warped first, then each pixel of the view sampled from the
+picture.
+@item layered
+The foreground's edges are drawn as a layer of their own over the
+background, which continues under them.
+@end table
+
+@item fill
+How the places the picture does not show (disocclusions) are filled.
+@table @samp
+@item background
+The background beside each hole is extended across it. This is the default.
+@item depth
+Only background pixels feed the fill, nearest first.
+@item telea
+Telea's fast marching inpainting, from the background side.
+@item none
+Holes are left black.
+@end table
+
+@item smooth
+Whether the depth is smoothed before warping.
+@table @samp
+@item off
+No smoothing. This is the default.
+@item gaussian
+An asymmetric Gaussian, stronger vertically, which shrinks the holes.
+@end table
+
+@item sigma_h
+@item sigma_v
+The horizontal and vertical standard deviations of the smoothing, in pixels
+of a picture 1920 wide. Default are 10 and 30.
+
+@item radius
+The neighbourhood of the depth and telea fills, in pixels. Default is 5.
+
+@item depth
+What a depth sample means: @samp{auto} (the default), @samp{nearness}
+(larger samples nearer), @samp{farness} (larger samples farther),
+@samp{inverse_z}, @samp{disparity}, @samp{z} and @samp{nonlinear} (the
+depth representation types 0 to 3), or @samp{parallax} (an ISO/IEC 23002-3
+parallax map).
+
+@item range
+For depth without absolute values, the largest parallax either side of the
+screen, in pixels of a picture 1920 wide. Default is 58, about one degree of
+visual angle for a viewer three picture heights away.
+
+@item zero
+For depth without absolute values, where the screen is, from 0 (the farthest
+samples) to 1 (the nearest). Default is 0.5.
+
+@item shift
+A parallax added to every point, in pixels of a picture 1920 wide, which
+moves the whole scene behind (positive) or in front of the screen. Default
+is 0.
+
+@item z_near
+@item z_far
+ZNear and ZFar for depth of type @samp{z} that carries none.
+@end table
+
+@subsection Examples
+@itemize
+@item
+Draw the right eye of a picture and its 2D-plus-depth map:
+@example
+ffmpeg -i picture.png -i depth.png -lavfi dibr stereo.png
+@end example
+
+@item
+Draw both eyes of an MPEG-2 transport stream's texture and its auxiliary
+depth stream, with the depth-aided fill:
+@example
+ffmpeg -i video.ts -lavfi "[0:v:0][0:v:1]dibr=view=center:fill=depth" stereo.mkv
+@end example
+@end itemize
+
 @section dilation
 
 Apply dilation effect to the video.
